{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00869 - MONDO_5338",
  "verbalization": "Dorzolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of open-angle glaucoma through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Dorzolamide may be associated with ocular hypertension, a precursor condition to open-angle glaucoma, and that this association is linked to latanoprost, a prostaglandin analog. The presence of cystoid macular edema as a side effect of Dorzolamide further supports a potential role in ocular pathology. The integration of these pathways implies that Dorzolamide may influence ocular physiology in a manner that contributes to the development of open-angle glaucoma, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The evidence is supported by a chain of indications and side effects that suggest a possible mechanistic link, though the precise biological pathways remain to be elucidated.",
